# Part 1 — Product & UX (PM hat)

## Updated design references (use these as source of truth)
User supplied newer design-system files in `~/Downloads` — these supersede the older `TrueKeeps Design System` folder where they overlap:
- `~/Downloads/app.jsx` — **new builder prototype**: quiz-first flow `quiz (occasion/vibe/title) → upload → generating → reveal ("pick a feel") → preview → customize | order → confirm`, with localStorage persistence, back-map navigation, layout-density regeneration, themes (ivory/cobalt/blush/gallery), cover styles, and a copy-voice toggle (warm/concise).
- `~/Downloads/landing.jsx` — new landing composition: `Nav → Hero → TrustStrip → HowItWorks → Showcase → Occasions → Proof → FinalCTA → Footer`, with IntersectionObserver scroll-reveal (incl. headless/no-IO fallback).
- `~/Downloads/_ds_bundle.js`, `_ds_manifest.json` (namespace `TrueKeepsDesignSystem_019dee`), `components.html` — updated TKLogo/TKMark/TKButton/TKBadge components + preview cards (hero pattern, cards, chips, inputs, wizard components, tokens).

**Product decision this implies:** adopt the quiz-first prototype flow as the primary (autopilot) path — it front-loads intent (occasion/vibe) so auto-generation can be smart, and it compresses the current 6-step wizard to 3 perceived steps with "customize" as an optional branch rather than a separate upfront path. The current 6-step wizard's components (BookSettings, PhotoUpload, etc.) get reused inside this flow, not thrown away.

## Guiding principle: Autopilot by default, customization on demand

Every step should produce a good result with **zero decisions** (smart defaults), and expose a single "Customize" affordance for power users. Concretely:

| Step | Autopilot (default) | Power-user escape hatch |
|---|---|---|
| Settings | Pre-selected best-seller size/pages/finish | Full option grid (already built) |
| Upload | AI curates: dedup, blur-cull, chronological order | Manual select/reorder, keep-all toggle |
| Layout/Theme | AI suggests theme from photo palette + occasion | Full theme grid + category filter (built) |
| Pages | Auto-generated spreads | **"Edit this page" → opens canvas editor scoped to that one spread** |
| Cover | Best-quality hero photo auto-picked | Photo picker + style variants (built) |
| Order | One-click checkout with saved details | Qty/shipping options |

The "Edit this page" bridge is the key missing link — it unifies the two paths (auto users never see the canvas; tinkerers drop in per-page) instead of forcing an upfront auto-vs-custom fork.

## Functionality gaps (ranked)

**P0 — cannot take money without these:**
1. **Real persistence** — Postgres wired up (Drizzle schema exists, MemStorage is used). Drafts die on server restart today.
2. **Object storage for photos** — base64-in-DB will not survive real usage (a 50-photo book ≈ 150MB+ of JSON). Presigned direct uploads to R2/S3 + thumbnails.
3. **Checkout & orders** — orders table, payment gateway, order-confirmation email, order status page. Guest checkout first (email-only), account optional.
4. **Print-ready export** — render each spread to 300-DPI PDF with bleed/trim marks. Without this there is literally no product to ship to a printer.
5. **Image pipeline** — HEIC conversion (iPhone users = majority of photo uploads), EXIF rotation, client-side compression before upload, server-side originals kept for print.

**P1 — experience gaps that will hurt conversion:**
6. **Photo reordering** in PageReview (drag to swap between pages) — the UI hints at it, it's unimplemented.
7. **Captions/text** — no text layer exists at all (not even in the schema). The design system's `book_builder/data.jsx` already has occasion presets with suggested titles + per-photo captions — port that data model into the app.
8. **Wire the dead controls** in create.tsx: position/size/rotation sliders (defaultValue only), layout-template buttons, Save, 3D preview, hardcoded "of 20" page count. **Dead UI is worse than missing UI** — it destroys trust.
9. **Apply the layout choice** — Step 3's layout style (classic/editorial/grid…) is stored but never affects page generation.
10. Duplicate detection, undo/redo, multi-draft support, shareable preview link ("show grandma before ordering" — also a viral loop).

**P2:** filters, stickers, promo codes, collaboration.

## Design gaps
- OrderSummary uses emoji ("Order placed 🎉") — violates the design system's no-emoji rule (Lucide only).
- The custom path: `?mode=custom` currently runs the identical 6 steps — it should route into the canvas editor after upload, per the spec.
- Canvas editor is desktop-only; wizard is mobile-first — accept this (edit on desktop, review/order on mobile) but detect and message it.
- Empty states, upload-failure recovery, and preflight warnings (photo will print blurry at this size / face falls in the gutter) are missing — preflight is a differentiator most competitors lack.

## AI strategy — "the best photobook, automatically"

Ordered by impact-per-effort:
1. **Smart curation (biggest win, cheapest):** perceptual-hash dedup + blur/exposure scoring + burst best-shot pick. Runs with `sharp` + a small heuristic model — no LLM needed, near-zero cost.
2. **Smart ordering & chaptering:** EXIF timestamps + GPS clustering → chronological chapters ("Day 1 — Rome"). Pure algorithm, no AI cost.
3. **Aspect-aware auto-layout:** pick spread templates by photo orientations/count instead of the current fixed grid. Algorithmic.
4. **Claude Vision for captions & titles** (batch, async): occasion detection, per-page captions, book title suggestions. Use Haiku 4.5 for cost (~$0.01–0.03/book with batching). This is the "wow" feature — market it as "AI writes your story."
5. **Theme suggestion from photo color palette** — extract dominant palette, map to nearest theme.
6. **Preflight "Make it better" pass:** one button that re-runs curation/layout and shows a before/after diff.

## Ordering & commerce
- Guest checkout with email; magic-link to reclaim drafts ("finish your book" recovery email = the highest-ROI email in this category, cart abandonment is huge for 30-min creation flows).
- Order state machine: `created → paid → rendering → sent_to_print → shipped → delivered`, webhook-driven, visible to the user.
- Reorder/reprint past books in one click (books are gifts; repeat prints are free margin).
- Payment gateway is region-dependent: Stripe Checkout if available; otherwise a local gateway (e.g. PayFast/Safepay for PK) behind the same `PaymentProvider` interface. **Assumption to confirm with user.**
- Print fulfillment: start with **manual PDF handoff to a local printer** (zero integration cost), design the pipeline so a print-API (Peecho/Gelato/Cloudprinter) can slot in later.

