# Part 2 — Architecture (Lead Architect hat)

## Stack decision: keep it, harden it
React 18 + Vite + TanStack Query + Express + Drizzle/Postgres is fine to 10k users. No rewrite. Add: object storage, a job queue, `sharp`, and a PDF renderer.

## Frontend
- **Kill base64.** Client compresses (`browser-image-compression`, in a Web Worker, keeps UI at 60fps during 100-photo uploads), then uploads **directly to object storage via presigned URLs** — files never transit the API server. Server only receives metadata.
- **Route-level code splitting:** `React.lazy` for `/wizard`, `/create`, `/templates` — the canvas editor (Framer Motion + full editor) shouldn't be in the landing bundle. Target <150KB gz initial.
- **Image delivery:** all grids/thumbnails load resized derivatives (200px/800px) via CDN, never originals; `loading="lazy"` + intersection observer; virtualize the photo library (`@tanstack/react-virtual`) — 300 uploaded photos must not mean 300 live DOM nodes.
- **Draft persistence:** move from localStorage (5MB cap, why photos are stripped today) to **IndexedDB** for local blobs + background sync of state to server. Draft survives refresh AND device switch (via email link).
- TanStack Query with optimistic updates for reorder/edit; debounced server autosave of wizard state.

## Backend
- **Postgres (Neon)** — schema mostly exists; add `orders`, `order_events`, `captions`/text-layer, `render_jobs`. Indexes on every FK used in lookups (`projectId`, `pageId`, `userId`).
- **N+1 elimination:** loading a project currently implies 1 + N(pages) + N×M(photos) queries. Replace with **one query per entity type using `inArray`** (3 queries total regardless of book size), assembled in memory. Add a `GET /api/projects/:id/full` endpoint returning the whole book in one round trip.
- **Object storage: Cloudflare R2** — S3-compatible, **zero egress fees** (photos are the dominant bandwidth cost; S3 egress would be the #1 bill at scale). Presigned PUT for upload, signed GET / public CDN for derivatives.
- **Job queue:** start with **pg-boss** (queue inside Postgres — no Redis to run/pay for). Jobs: thumbnail generation (`sharp`), HEIC fallback conversion, AI caption batches, **PDF rendering**, emails. Swap to BullMQ+Redis only when job volume demands it (~10k tier); same handler code.
- **PDF rendering:** render spreads server-side with the same layout math as the client → 300-DPI CMYK-safe PDF via headless Chromium (`puppeteer` printing the actual React page templates = pixel-perfect WYSIWYG, one layout codebase) with `pdf-lib` for imposition/bleed/trim marks. Runs as a queue job, never in a request handler.
- **Auth:** session-based (deps already installed: `express-session` + `connect-pg-simple`), guest sessions promoted to accounts at checkout via email magic link.
- **API hardening:** zod validation on every route (schemas exist via `drizzle-zod`), rate limiting, upload size/MIME validation, all photo access authorized by project ownership.

## Infra & scaling tiers

| | ~100 users | ~1k users | ~10k users |
|---|---|---|---|
| App | 1 small VPS or Railway/Fly instance (API + worker in one process) | Same host, **worker split to own process** | 2–3 API replicas behind LB; autoscaled worker pool for renders |
| DB | Neon free tier | Neon Launch (~$19) | Neon Scale + connection pooling (built-in pgbouncer) |
| Storage/CDN | R2 + Cloudflare CDN (free tier CDN) | same | same (this is why R2: egress stays $0) |
| Queue | pg-boss in Postgres | pg-boss | BullMQ + managed Redis |
| Cost/mo | **~$5–15** | **~$40–80** | **~$300–600** |

- PDF render (Chromium) is the only heavy compute — it's queued and horizontally scalable independently of the API, so a launch-day order spike queues renders instead of taking the site down.
- Observability from day one: Sentry (FE+BE, free tier), pino structured logs, uptime check on `/health`. At 1k+, add basic metrics (queue depth, render duration, p95 latency).
- Deploy: Dockerfile + GitHub Actions → Railway/Fly (or Hetzner + Coolify if optimizing cost). Env-based config already started in `.env.example`.

